### Deep Dive Analysis: The "Smoking Gun" in v2.4.1

I compared the source code of **v2.4.0** and **v2.4.1** to understand why this crash is appearing now, even though the Kysely version (0.28.2) remains the same.

The regression was introduced in `server/src/services/asset.service.ts` via the new automatic timezone extraction logic.

#### Code Difference

In **v2.4.0**, the database was only called if an explicit `timeZone` was provided:

```typescript
if (timeZone !== undefined) {
  await this.assetRepository.updateDateTimeOriginal(ids, dateTimeRelative, timeZone);
}
```

In **v2.4.1**, the logic was changed to support automatic extraction from `dateTimeOriginal`. Crucially, it now passes a value that can be **`undefined`** directly into the repository call:

```typescript
const extractedTimeZone = dateTimeOriginal ? DateTime.fromISO(dateTimeOriginal, { setZone: true }).zone : undefined;
// ...
if (
  (dateTimeRelative !== undefined && dateTimeRelative !== 0) ||
  timeZone !== undefined ||
  extractedTimeZone?.type === 'fixed'
) {
  // timeZone ?? extractedTimeZone?.name can result in 'undefined' being passed as the 3rd argument
  await this.assetRepository.updateDateTimeOriginal(ids, dateTimeRelative, timeZone ?? extractedTimeZone?.name);
}
```

#### Why this triggers the crash

The `assetRepository.updateDateTimeOriginal` method uses a Kysely template literal (`sql\``). When`undefined` is passed as a parameter to these templates, `kysely@0.28.2` fails during the query compilation phase in `DefaultQueryCompiler.visitValue`, leading to the`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'val')`.

#### Verification

This perfectly explains why **v2.4.0 is stable** while **v2.4.1 crashes**: The latent bug in the Kysely compiler wasn't reachable via this code path in the previous version.

I recommend either:

1. Updating the Kysely compiler to handle `undefined` values (e.g. converting them to `null`).
2. Ensuring that Repository methods sanitize `undefined` inputs before passing them to the `sql` template literal.

I hope this helps in fixing the regression!

@jrasm91 not sure if I understood fully what you mean, but after applying your suggestions, we lose information in the generated spec, see https://github.com/immich-app/immich/pull/25185/commits/6a1991058cd33df18ddb48f528695e7ecb041f29


from what I see from my test in that commit and some ressearch:
> OpenAPI has operation-level fields that DTOs can’t populate. requestBody.description and parameter descriptions come from @ApiBody / @ApiParam; moving docs to DTO JSDoc drops that data, as seen in the patch.
JSDoc is descriptive text, not schema control. It can’t reliably express OpenAPI constraints (e.g. integer vs number, formats, nullability), which is why types regressed when explicit Swagger metadata was removed.
Type inference also can’t infer human-facing descriptions. Those strings were explicit decorator metadata.
So relying only on JSDoc + inference reduces duplication, but also reduces OpenAPI fidelity. For deterministic output—operation docs, request/param descriptions, and precise schema typing—explicit Swagger metadata is still required.

